
==========================================================================
1. THE SOLAR PATH, PRICED FROM FIRST PRINCIPLES BEFORE ANY RUN
==========================================================================
  Solar ERF (CMIP7/FaIR 1.6.0): 11-yr amplitude 0.0378 W/m2, and the
  EARLY-20TH-CENTURY RISE Marcus named IS THERE: 1900-19 -> 1950-69 = +0.0267 W/m2.

  ERF -> TSI (ERF = dTSI/4 x (1 - 0.29)) -> fraction of 1361 W/m2:
    11-yr, peak-to-trough  0.426 W/m2 TSI = 0.0313 % of TSI
    secular 1900s->1950s   0.150 W/m2 TSI = 0.0110 % of TSI

  UPPER BOUND on NPP, taking NPP PROPORTIONAL to irradiance (it is not --
  photosynthesis saturates with light, so 1:1 is a CEILING), on 52.7 PgC/yr:
    11-yr amplitude  0.0082 GtC/yr   = 0.91 % of the observed anomaly sd (0.911)
                                   = 0.0041 % of its VARIANCE
    secular shift    0.0058 GtC/yr   -> 0.29 GtC over the 50 yr it takes

  FOR SCALE, the mechanism the model ALREADY HAS: gamma_diff 0.25 x Pinatubo
  AOD 0.110 = 2.74 % of NPP = 1.445 GtC/yr -- 175x the solar 11-yr bound.

  ⇒ THE STRUCTURAL REASON, not just the arithmetic: aerosols change the
    DIFFUSE FRACTION, which redistributes light into shaded canopy; the sun
    changes TOTAL irradiance, which is 1:1 at best. The high-leverage
    channel is UNAVAILABLE to solar by physics, and the low-leverage one is
    three orders of magnitude down in VARIANCE. No run can rescue that.

==========================================================================
2. ANTHROPOGENIC AEROSOL -> DIFFUSE LIGHT: THE SHAPE FIRST
==========================================================================
  CMIP7 SO2: 1750 2.3 -> PEAK 139.9 Mt SO2/yr in 1979 -> 71.8 in 2024.
  ⛔ AND IT IS SMOOTH: interannual sd about a 12-dof spline is 3.79 Mt,
     2.7 % of the peak. Sulfate lifetime is days, so anthropogenic AOD
     inherits the emissions' shape -- a TREND driver, not an interannual one.
     ⇒ Predicted before running: this helps GATE V hardly at all. What it
       could move is the AF TREND and the decadal profile.

