5. ⭐ WHAT BREAKS THE DEGENERACY: FACE, NOT THE CO2 RECORD
==========================================================================
  FACE observations, 400 -> 564 ppm: Norby 2025 synthesis 21.8 %; Norby 2005 (young plantations) 23.0 %
  ⚠ Those are YOUNG PLANTATIONS; the mature-forest end is lower, so this
    reads as a CEILING on the model's response, not a target.

  A_peak      beta_max    fert_k R(400-564) vs Norby 21.8%
  0.00          0.6974     303.7      11.5 %      -10.3 pp
  0.02          0.7547     351.5      12.0 %       -9.8 pp
  0.05          0.8975     465.1      13.0 %       -8.8 pp
  0.10          1.6339    1026.8      15.5 %       -6.3 pp
  0.20          2.4085    2000.0      14.3 %       -7.5 pp

  ⇒ FACE NARROWS IT BUT DOES NOT DECIDE IT, and saying so is the point. The
    compensated response moves +4 pp, which stays INSIDE a FACE envelope that
    runs from 0 % (EucFACE, mature and P-limited) to 23 % (young plantations)
    -- an envelope too wide to reject anything here. So the honest reading is:
    the CO2 record cannot identify the term, and neither can FACE.
  ⚠ WHAT IS STILL TRUE, AND IS THE ARGUMENT AGAINST ADDING IT NOW: buying the
    aerosol term RELOCATES `beta_max` by a factor of 2.3 while changing nothing
    measurable -- and `beta_max` is what the pulse IRF and every fertilisation
    result rest on. An unidentifiable term that silently moves an identified
    parameter is a bad trade. [[ccx_saturating_fert]], [[ccx_beta_recentred]]

